## 3. Detection

- **What alerted you?** CloudWatch `CPUUtilization` metric, cross-checked manually via the CloudWatch console graph (SNS email notification).
- **What metric/threshold fired?** Alarm condition: `CPUUtilization > 70 for 3 datapoints within 15 minutes`.
- **Time to detect:** Visible on the CloudWatch graph within the same 5-minute reporting window as the load, though the alarm itself did not transition to "In alarm" during the first run due to the evaluation-period gap described below.

---

## 4. Investigation

- **Tools/commands used:**
  - `ps aux | grep stress` — confirmed the `stress` process and its child workers, along with real-time CPU percentage per process
  - CloudWatch console graph (CPUUtilization, 3h view) — confirmed the metric itself was spiking correctly, up to 99.31%
  - Manual comparison between the alarm's configured evaluation rule and the observed metric pattern

- **What was ruled out:** The lack of an "In alarm" state was **not** caused by the stress test failing to load the CPU — `ps aux` and the CloudWatch graph both confirmed genuine spikes up to ~99%. It also was not a security group or instance-level issue.

- **Root cause identified:** The alarm's evaluation period (`3 datapoints within 15 minutes`, i.e., CPU must stay above 70% across three consecutive 5-minute samples) was not compatible with a short, intermittent stress run. The load was real and measurable, but not *sustained* long enough in an unbroken window to satisfy the alarm's M-of-N condition. This is a common real-world alarm-tuning pitfall — a metric can clearly breach a threshold without the alarm ever firing, if the evaluation period doesn't match the actual failure pattern being monitored.

---

## 5. Resolution

- **Action taken:** Re-ran the stress test as a longer, continuous load (stress --cpu 2 --timeout 1200) to span multiple full 5-minute evaluation windows without interruption, rather than the shorter 180-second burst used in the first attempt. No changes were made to the alarm's threshold or datapoints-to-alarm configuration — the existing 3-of-3/15-minute rule fired correctly once the load pattern matched it.
- **Verification:** Confirmed via `ps aux` that both stress worker processes were actively consuming ~99.9% CPU each during the run.
- **Time to resolve:** 07:30 (alarm triggered) → 07:32 (CPU and alarm both returned to baseline) — approximately 2 minutes from alarm trigger to full recovery.
---

## 6. Impact

- **Who/what was affected:** Single lab EC2 instance only; no external users or production systems involved.
- **Duration of degraded state:** Approximately 15–20 minutes across both stress test attempts, as part of an intentional test — not an unplanned outage.

---

## 7. Prevention / Follow-up Actions

- [ ] When configuring CPU alarms going forward, match the "datapoints to alarm" evaluation window to the realistic duration of the failure mode being monitored — a brief spike-and-recover pattern needs a shorter or lower-datapoint-count alarm than a sustained-load scenario.
- [ ] Add a secondary alarm with a shorter evaluation period (e.g., 1 datapoint over 1 minute) specifically to catch brief spikes, alongside the existing sustained-load alarm — this mirrors how production environments often layer multiple alarms at different sensitivities for the same metric.
- [ ] Document the distinction between "metric breached threshold" and "alarm fired" for future reference — they are not the same thing, and conflating them is an easy mistake under incident pressure.
- [ ] Confirm SNS email subscription is actually confirmed/active for future test runs, so alerts arrive in real time rather than requiring manual console checks.

---

## 8. Lessons Learned

This exercise didn't go as initially planned — the first stress run clearly spiked CPU, but the alarm never fired, which actually taught the more valuable lesson. CloudWatch alarms don't trigger on a single breach; they require a sustained pattern matching the configured evaluation period. A monitoring setup that *looks* correct on paper can still miss real spikes if the evaluation window doesn't match the actual failure pattern. This is directly applicable to real cloud support work: tuning alarm sensitivity isn't a one-time setup step, it needs to match the specific type of incident you're trying to catch.

